@q 40:11-13

The **liver** is the **largest gland**: it stores glucose as **glycogen**, makes **bile**, detoxifies alcohol and drugs, makes urea and stores vitamins A, D and B_{12}. **Gallstones** block bile flow and so hurt **fat** digestion.

@section 22.3 | Respiration

@q 42:1-4

Gas exchange happens in the **alveoli** (about 300 million tiny air sacs). The breathing rate is controlled by the **medulla**, which responds mainly to **rising CO_{2}** and falling oxygen in blood. Cells store energy in **ATP**; splitting **ATP → ADP** releases it (about 7.3 kcal per mole).

@section 22.4 | Heart and Blood

@q 43:1-4

The human heart has **four chambers**: two atria and two ventricles. **Pulmonary veins** are the only veins with **oxygenated** blood (lungs → left atrium), and the **pulmonary artery** is the only artery with deoxygenated blood. The heart rests only **between beats** (diastole), about 0.4 s of each 0.8 s cycle at 72 beats a minute. The **pulse** is the same as the heart rate.

@pyq U.P. R.O./A.R.O. (Pre) 2017
The total amount of blood present in a normal human (70 kg) adult is about
(a) 6000 ml
(b) 5000 ml
(c) 4000ml
(d) 3000 ml
@ans (*)
@note Blood is about **7-8% of body weight**, so a 70 kg adult has roughly **5-5.6 litres**. Both (a) and (b) can be defended, so the source marks the question (*); **5 litres** is the usual textbook figure.
@end
@src 43:5

@q 43:6-10

@table
Blood component | Fact
**Plasma** (about 55%) | 90% water + proteins (albumin, globulin, fibrinogen), salts, glucose
**RBC** (erythrocytes) | Made in **red bone marrow**; no nucleus in mammals; live about **120 days**; destroyed in the **spleen** ("graveyard of RBCs") and liver
**Haemoglobin** | **Iron**-containing red pigment; carries **O_{2}** (and some CO_{2}); slightly **alkaline**
**WBC** (leucocytes) | **Immunity**; made in bone marrow and lymphoid tissue (spleen, lymph nodes)
**Platelets** | **Clotting** (with thrombin and fibrinogen)
**Blood pH** | **7.35-7.45** (slightly alkaline)
Blood glucose | In **mg/dL** (fasting 70-100)
@end

Blood is a **fluid connective tissue**. Haemoglobin and **myoglobin** (muscle) contain iron; chlorophyll contains **magnesium**; keratin and collagen are structural proteins. Immunity comes from WBCs and antibodies, not haemoglobin.

@q 43:11-12

Normal blood pressure is about **120/80 mm Hg** (systolic/diastolic), where **Hg is mercury**, measured with a **sphygmomanometer**.

@q 43:13-14

@table
Blood group | Antigen on RBC | Antibody in plasma | Can give to | Can receive from
A | A | Anti-B | A, AB | A, O
B | B | Anti-A | B, AB | B, O
AB | A and B | None | AB | **All** (**universal recipient**)
**O** | None | Anti-A and anti-B | **All** (**universal donor**) | O
@end

**Karl Landsteiner** discovered the ABO groups (1900) and, with Wiener, the **Rh factor** (1940). In an emergency **O negative** is safest. India's **e-RaktKosh** tracks blood banks.

@q 43:15-20

**Thrombin** converts fibrinogen to fibrin in **clotting**. **Alum's** aluminium ions **coagulate** the colloidal blood proteins, which is why it stops bleeding from shaving cuts. Sheffield University (UK) researchers developed a plastic **artificial blood** (2007).

@section 22.5 | Excretion

@q 44:1-8

@table
Kidney fact | Detail
Unit | **Nephron** (about a million per kidney)
Job | **Filters all the blood** (about 180 L of filtrate a day), removes **urea**, balances water and salts, makes hormones (**erythropoietin**, renin) and activates vitamin D
Urine | About **1.5 litres** a day; slightly acidic; yellow from **urochrome**
Urea | Made in the **liver**, removed by the **kidneys**
**Kidney stone** | Mostly **calcium oxalate** crystals
**Dialysis** | Artificial kidney (dialyser) filters blood when kidneys fail
@end

Blood sugar is regulated by **insulin and glucagon** from the pancreas, not by the kidney; the **liver** detoxifies alcohol.

